    {
      "filename": "Rainforest orchid (3455011351).jpg",
      "title": "File:Rainforest orchid (3455011351).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/Rainforest_orchid_%283455011351%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rainforest_orchid_(3455011351).jpg",
      "width": 1024,
      "height": 768,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "Tatiana Gerus from Brisbane, Australia",
      "description": "The fallen tree took down everything around, so all upper rainforest levels including the canopy were on the ground. You could find a lot of interesting plants (ferns, orchids...) here, what normally would be far up in the trees.\nYou can see on the foreground Thelychiton speciosus syn. Dendrobium speciosum\nCommon names: Rock orchid, King orchid, Cedarvale orchids\nasgap.org.au/d-spe.html\nID credit to Füzzy Mijmark.\nWidespread Australian orchid, perhaps the most popular orchid in the world today.(Ross Harvey). It is easy to grow in tropical to temperate climates. It can be grown on branches of trees or on rocks.\nThe showy flowers grow in long racemes on straight or slowly arching, long, starchy stems with over 100 flowers per stem from August to October. Their colour varies from white to creamy yellow.\n\nLocation: Goomburra section of Main Range National Park, Queensland, Australia",
      "dateCreated": "2009-04-12 12:59",
      "categories": [
        "CC-BY-2.0",
